Potential bug: In the prefix check, we skip if L[i-1] == INF. But what if i>0 and the prefix can be matched but L[i-1] might be INF because the greedy left matching failed to match the prefix? That would mean the prefix itself cannot be matched naturally, which is correct because if prefix cannot be matched naturally, then even with replacement at i, we cannot match prefix (since replacement is used at i). So skip is correct.

Similarly for suffix.

But consider case where prefix can be matched naturally but the greedy left matching might have matched the prefix in a way that uses some t positions that overlap with the suffix matching in a way that makes the interval empty even though a different matching of prefix could allow a gap? However, using the earliest possible prefix matching (L) and latest possible suffix matching (R) gives the maximum possible gap. If even with the best case (earliest prefix end and latest suffix start) there is no gap (i.e., left_bound+1 >= right_bound), then no matching exists because any matching of prefix must end at or after L[i-1] (since L gives earliest end), and any matching of suffix must start at or before R[i+1] (since R gives latest start). Thus if earliest prefix end is not strictly before latest suffix start, then no p exists that can be placed between. So condition is necessary and sufficient.

